        "content": "Documents give different answers about the same thing. Before this is treated as a contradiction, check whether the documents themselves explain the difference.\n\nQuestion: Who is the artist of the album \"Feel the Fire\"?\n\nAnswer A: Family Brown\n  about: Family Brown\n  sense: album\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Feel the Fire is the tenth studio album by Canadian country music group Family Brown.\" [document 1]\n\nAnswer B: Feel the Fire\n  about: Family Brown\n  sense: album\n  as of: not stated\n  kind: factual\n  independent sources: 1\n  evidence: \"Feel the Fire ( Family Brown album )\" [document 2]\n\nDocuments:\nDocument 1: Feel the Fire (Family Brown album) Feel the Fire is the tenth studio album by Canadian country music group Family Brown. It was released in 1985 by RCA Records and was the final album to include founding member Joe Brown, who died in 1986. The album includes the singles, \"Feel the Fire\", \"What If It's Right\", \"Wouldn't You Love Us Together Again\", and \"I Love You More\", which all charted on the \"RPM\" Country Tracks chart in Canada. The album won the awards for Album of the Year at the 1985 \"RPM\" Big Country Awards and the 1986 Canadian Country\nDocument 2: the Terms of Use and Privacy Policy . Wikipedia® is a registered trademark of the Wikimedia Foundation , Inc. , a non-profit organization . Privacy policy About Wikipedia Disclaimers Contact Wikipedia Code of Conduct Developers Statistics Cookie statement Mobile view Search Search Toggle the table of contents Feel the Fire ( Family Brown album ) Add languages Add topic\nDocument 4: Feel the Fire (Jermaine Jackson album) Feel the Fire is the fourth solo album by Jermaine Jackson, and his second post-Jackson 5 solo album. It was dedicated: \"to Jai, born January 27, 1977\". This is the first album for which Jackson did some producing and writing for himself. The album includes Tower of Power's horn section, and Stevie Wonder's ex-wife Syreeta. Its only single release was the Earth, Wind & Fire-inspired \"You Need To Be Loved\", which has a saxophone solo by Lenny Pickett. Executive producer: Berry Gordy Arrangers: Greg Adams, Greg Wright, Clay Drayton, Don Peake, Benjamin Wright, Gil\nDocument 5: Jermaine Jackson 0 references publication date 29 June 1977 0 references record label Motown 1 reference imported from Wikimedia project English Wikipedia producer Jermaine Jackson 1 reference imported from Wikimedia project English Wikipedia Identifiers Discogs master ID 119970 1 reference stated in MusicBrainz retrieved 3 September 2015 Freebase ID /m/0cc82_n 0 references MusicBrainz release group ID 667fc091-a1ae-4d31-8ac5-8bb80a18fa60 1 reference stated in MusicBrainz Sitelinks Wikipedia ( 3 entries ) edit enwiki Feel the Fire ( Jermaine Jackson album ) itwiki Feel the Fire ( Jermaine Jackson ) plwiki Feel the Fire Wikibooks ( 0 entries ) edit Wikinews ( 0 entries\n\nLook for a sentence in the documents which shows that the answers do not conflict, because\n- they refer to different senses: different events, editions, versions, conditions, places or measures (an original release and a re-release, for example), or\n- they refer to different times: the value changed, and each answer was right at its own date.\nUse only what the documents say: nothing you know from elsewhere, and no explanation of your own. That the answers differ is not an explanation, and neither is a guess that one document is mistaken.\n\nReply with JSON only. If a sentence explains the difference:\n{\"explained\": true, \"by\": \"sense\", \"document\": 1, \"quote\": \"\", \"labels\": {\"A\": \"\", \"B\": \"\"}}\n\"by\" is \"sense\" or \"time\". \"quote\" is that sentence, copied word for word, and \"document\" is the number of the document it is in. \"labels\" gives, for each answer, the sense or the date it refers to.\nIf no sentence explains it:\n{\"explained\": false}\n\nInside a JSON string, write